WEBVTT

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:03.700
JavaScript applications process untrusted data constantly.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:08.165
A browser application receives form fields, query strings, storage values, messages,

00:00:08.165 --> 00:00:10.300
files, and data from third-party services.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:14.568
A Node.js service receives request bodies, route parameters, headers, cookies,

00:00:14.568 --> 00:00:18.600
WebSocket messages, environment variables, queue events, and API responses.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.065
Input validation is the defensive boundary that asks whether a value is acceptable before

00:00:23.065 --> 00:00:26.065
the application trusts it or uses it to make decisions.

00:00:26.065 --> 00:00:30.010
JavaScript's flexibility is productive, but it also creates validation risk.

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:34.265
Dynamic typing and type coercion can make values behave in unexpected ways if checks are

00:00:34.265 --> 00:00:35.345
loose or ambiguous.

00:00:35.345 --> 00:00:39.000
A string may be compared like a number, an empty value may become false,

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.600
an array may be accepted where an object was expected,

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.980
and a missing field may move through code until it causes unsafe behavior.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:48.000
Clear runtime validation makes those assumptions explicit.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.380
Validation is related to other controls, but it is not the same thing.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.850
Parsing turns raw input into structured data.

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:57.045
Normalization converts data into a consistent representation.

00:00:57.045 --> 00:01:01.700
Sanitization changes data, often to remove or transform unwanted parts.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:06.455
Output encoding prepares data for a specific output context such as HTML, JavaScript,

00:01:06.455 --> 00:01:08.700
URLs, logs, or SQL parameters.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:12.200
Validation decides whether the application should accept the value at all.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.800
Input validation is one layer of a secure design.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:18.445
It does not replace authorization, output encoding, parameterized queries,

00:01:18.445 --> 00:01:21.895
safe file handling, safe command execution, or business logic checks.

00:01:21.895 --> 00:01:23.560
Its value is predictability.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.755
When JavaScript applications validate inputs at the right boundaries,

00:01:26.755 --> 00:01:30.280
they reduce surprises before untrusted data reaches sensitive code.
